'''brevet''' (brē-vet′), ''n.'' and ''a.'' [< ME. ''brevet'', a letter of indulgence, < OF. ''brievet'', F. ''brevet'' (ML. ''brevetum''), a commission, license, etc., lit. a short writing, dim. of OF. ''brief'', F. ''bref'', a writing: see ''brief''.] '''I.''' ''n.'' '''1†.''' A letter of authority; a commission.
{{smaller block|<poem>I wol go fecche my box with my ''breuettes''
And a bulle with bisshopes lettres.</poem>
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), v. 649.}}}}
'''2.''' In the British and American armies, a commission to an officer which promotes him to a higher rank, without conferring a right to receive corresponding advance in pay. {{smaller|In Great Britain it does not descend lower than the rank of captain, nor ascend higher than that of lieutenant-colonel, and confers the right to a corresponding advance in command. In the United States army it extends from the rank of first lieutenant to that of lieutenant-general, but gives no advanced command except by special assignment of the President. Brevets are conferred by and with the advice and consent of the Senate for “gallant actions and meritorious services.”}}
{{smaller block|They give ''brevets'' to majors and captains to act as colonels in the army.
{{right|''Swift'', Journal to Stella, Letter 61.}}}}
'''3.''' A patent; a warrant; a license; a commission; an official diploma in writing, conferring some privilege or distinction. [French usages.]

'''II.''' ''a.'' Assigned or conferred by brevet; appointed by brevet.
{{smaller block|What is called ''brevet'' rank is given to officers of all branches of the army as a reward for brilliant and lengthened service; and when such nominal rank has been held for a certain number of years, it is usually converted into substantial rank.
{{right|''A. Fonblanque, Jr.'', How we are Governed.}}}}

